WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:02.969
-Fred,
it's always so great to see you,

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:04.670
and it's always so great to have
you back here in New York City.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:05.838
You travel the world.

00:00:05.838 --> 00:00:08.707
I know you love
everywhere you go,

00:00:08.707 --> 00:00:10.276
but you're a New Yorker,
through and through.

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:11.978
-All the way.
-All the way.

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.879
And you know,
this city right now,

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:16.782
one of the things that's sort of
collecting a bad reputation

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:19.386
is the subway,
and I know a lot of people

00:00:19.386 --> 00:00:20.987
are trying to come up
with solutions

00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:22.621
for what's ailing the subway,

00:00:22.621 --> 00:00:26.091
but you actually,
I heard you today --

00:00:26.091 --> 00:00:27.861
you say you have an alternative
to the subway.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.329
-I know where you're
going with this, yeah.

00:00:29.329 --> 00:00:31.597
Yeah.

00:00:31.597 --> 00:00:33.500
-[ Laughs ] Go ahead.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.667
[ Laughter ]

00:00:34.667 --> 00:00:37.537
-I get asked about it a lot.
-Yeah.

00:00:37.537 --> 00:00:39.171
-The problem is the tracks.

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:42.341
That's what we don't look at,
is the actual tracks.

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:45.345
They're expensive.
They're hard to maintain.

00:00:45.345 --> 00:00:46.879
So what we're
trying to do is,

00:00:46.879 --> 00:00:48.181
instead of having, like,

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:49.883
one subway go down
the middle of a tunnel,

00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:52.685
why don't we have
a couple side-by-side?

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:54.520
And like Uber, like a taxi,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:56.189
you get to tell
the subway driver,

00:00:56.189 --> 00:00:58.624
"Hey, I'm going to this street,
I'm going to that one.

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:00.159
Can you take me there?"

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:01.294
They can double back.

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:02.295
They can go --

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:03.562
It's a little --

00:01:03.562 --> 00:01:05.164
You know,
that's from another time.

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:07.800
That's from, like, the '30s,
when things were very like this.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:08.968
This is a different time.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.469
Let's get these trains
out there.

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:13.640
Let's get it all loose,
you know what I'm saying?

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:14.973
We've got to stop --
-So, wow.

00:01:14.973 --> 00:01:18.178
This seems like it's gonna make
it a bigger problem,

00:01:18.178 --> 00:01:22.148
because like, right now,
each train goes one place

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:23.549
and they're having
a huge problem with this.

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:25.919
And you would say,
your solution is,

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:29.088
every train could go
wherever the people...

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:31.257
So it's not like, you don't even
look at a map anymore

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.192
to say, like, "Oh, I need the
Orange line or the Green line

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:34.293
or the Red Line"?

00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:36.196
-Yeah, but it's something
to be discussed.

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:37.730
That's what the problem is.

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.465
They're not talking.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:44.370
There's no talking going on
with the subway companies.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.238
-See, right away,
I think one of the problems is,

00:01:46.238 --> 00:01:48.140
you're saying things like
"the subway companies."

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.375
Like there's...
[ Laughter ]

00:01:49.375 --> 00:01:50.676
Like there's more than one,

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:52.678
makes me think that maybe
you haven't done enough talking.

00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:54.414
-No, maybe not.

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:56.148
[ Laughter ]

00:01:56.148 --> 00:01:57.216
-You know what?

00:01:57.216 --> 00:01:58.818
You know what makes
you different

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:00.453
than the politicians, Freddy?

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:01.921
You're willing to look inward
and say,

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:03.256
"You know what?
I maybe got this one wrong."

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:04.724
-Yeah, yeah.

00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:06.326
Maybe.

00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:12.131
[ Laughter ]

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:13.499
-Our guest tonight is --

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.168
Give it up for Fred
one more time.

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:17.403
[ Cheers and applause ]

